// English versions of the cocktail "story" blurbs in cocktailStories.ko.ts —
// origin/history plus a short taste + occasion note, for each cocktail in
// src/data/cocktails.ts. Written as a faithful adaptation of the Korean
// originals (same claims, same hedging on disputed origins), not independent
// research, so keep the two files in sync: a correction to one (e.g. a
// creator's name) should be checked against the other. Historical details are
// written to the best of available knowledge; where a cocktail's origin is
// genuinely disputed among bartenders and historians, the text says so rather
// than asserting one unverified claim as settled fact.
export const COCKTAIL_STORY_EN: Record<string, string> = {
  'old-fashioned': 'Most accounts trace the Old Fashioned to the Pendennis Club in Louisville, Kentucky, in the 19th century, making it one of the oldest and most iconic cocktails around. Its name is said to reflect a commitment to the simple, "old-fashioned" way of making it, and it saw a major resurgence in modern times thanks to the TV show Mad Men. What defines it is whiskey\'s own character, lightly touched with sugar and bitters, for an honest, unfussy flavor.',
  'manhattan': 'The most widely accepted account places the Manhattan\'s birth at Manhattan\'s own club scene in the late 19th century, with one colorful story even linking its creation to Winston Churchill\'s mother. Rye whiskey\'s spice, sweet vermouth\'s gentle sweetness, and bitters\' bittersweet edge come together in perfect balance, making it the quintessential whiskey cocktail.',
  'whiskey-sour': 'One of the oldest and most iconic sour cocktails, dating back to the mid-19th century, the Whiskey Sour is praised for its perfect balance of whiskey, lemon, and sugar. Adding egg white gives it an even smoother, richer foam, making it just as fitting as an aperitif.',
  'boulevardier': 'As the story goes, the Boulevardier was created in 1920s Paris by Erskine Gwynne, an American journalist who named it after the magazine he\'d founded. It reworks a Negroni by swapping gin for bourbon, giving it a fuller, warmer character than its gin-based cousin.',
  'rob-roy': 'Created in 1894 at New York\'s Waldorf Hotel, the Rob Roy was named after the operetta of the same name playing on Broadway at the time. It reworks the Manhattan by swapping rye whiskey for Scotch, giving it a striking smoky character.',
  'sazerac': 'Born in 19th-century New Orleans, the Sazerac is counted among the oldest cocktails in the world and remains the official cocktail of the city to this day. Its distinctive method, rinsing the glass with absinthe for aroma, along with the signature scent of Peychaud\'s bitters, captures the essence of New Orleans cocktail culture.',
  'mint-julep': 'A time-honored classic that traces back to the American South in the 18th century, the Mint Julep became known around the world as the official drink of the Kentucky Derby. The tradition of packing a frosted silver cup with crushed mint and bourbon lives on today, and its cool, fragrant flavor makes it a natural fit for a summer evening gathering.',
  'vieux-carr': 'Created in the 1930s at the storied Hotel Monteleone in New Orleans, the Vieux Carré takes its name from the city\'s old French Quarter. Rye whiskey and cognac come together with Benedictine for the deep, complex flavor characteristic of New Orleans cocktails.',
  'rusty-nail': 'Popular in the United States and Britain in the 1960s, the Rusty Nail is said to owe its name to the drink once being stirred with an actual rusty nail. Made from just Scotch and honeyed Drambuie liqueur, it delivers a rich, intense flavor.',
  'new-york-sour': 'A classic that emerged in the United States in the late 19th century, the New York Sour is known for its signature gradient: a float of red wine gently layered over a whiskey sour. The wine\'s subtle tannic edge adds an extra layer of depth to the drink\'s flavor.',
  'paper-plane': 'A modern classic created in 2007 by New York bartender Sam Ross, who named it after M.I.A.\'s song "Paper Planes." Bourbon, amaro, Aperol, and lemon juice come together in equal parts, striking a bittersweet balance that has made it one of the defining craft cocktails of the 21st century.',
  'penicillin': 'A modern classic created in 2005 by New York bartender Sam Ross, said to have gotten its name from a joke that it would be just the thing to drink when you have a cold. The warm flavors of honey and ginger meet a smoky Scotch whisky float, resulting in a deep, complex sip.',
  'gold-rush': 'A modern classic created in the early 2000s by T.J. Siegel at Milk & Honey in New York, it rounds out a whiskey sour with honey for a smoother finish. With only three ingredients, its finely tuned balance has made it a steady favorite in craft cocktail bars.',
  'irish-coffee': 'This drink is famous for the story of a chef at Shannon Airport in Ireland in the 1940s, who made it for travelers worn out by the cold weather. Hot coffee is layered with Irish whiskey and soft cream, making it a fitting way to end a winter evening.',
  'hot-toddy': 'One of the oldest cocktails around, the Hot Toddy is said to have started as a folk remedy for colds and cold weather in 18th-century Scotland and England. Warm whisky is rounded out with honey and lemon, making it a cozy way to close out a chilly evening with friends.',
  'whiskey-smash': 'A modern-style bourbon sour made with plenty of muddled mint and lemon, the Whiskey Smash became hugely popular at craft cocktail bars from the 2000s onward. Refreshing mint aroma and bourbon\'s sweetness come together in harmony.',
  'whiskey-highball': 'Popularized in Britain and the United States in the early 20th century, the Whiskey Highball has recently surged back into popularity worldwide alongside the rise of Japanese-style highball culture. Made with just whiskey and cold soda water, it\'s crisp and endlessly drinkable.',
  'old-pal': 'As the story goes, the Old Pal\'s name comes from a friendship among American journalists in 1920s Paris, and it\'s a drier take on the Boulevardier. Rye whiskey\'s spice balances against Campari\'s bitterness.',
  'ward-8': 'This classic is famous for the story that it was created to celebrate a Boston politician\'s election victory in the late 19th century. Orange and lemon come together for a bright, vivid citrus flavor.',
  'blood-and-sand': 'Said to have been created to mark the 1922 release of the film of the same name starring Rudolph Valentino, Blood and Sand is one of the rare sour-style cocktails built around Scotch whisky. Cherry liqueur and orange bring a fruity sweetness that softens the smokiness of the Scotch.',
  'dry-martini': 'An iconic cocktail hailed as the "king of cocktails" since the early 20th century, the Dry Martini is built from little more than gin and a whisper of dry vermouth. Churchill is said to have joked that merely glancing at the vermouth bottle was enough, a nod to just how dry and intense the drink can be.',
  'negroni': 'This cocktail is famous for the story that in 1919 in Florence, Italy, Count Camillo Negroni asked for soda water in his usual Americano to be replaced with gin. Gin, Campari, and sweet vermouth are combined in exactly equal parts for a bitter yet perfectly balanced drink, and it stands as one of the defining aperitifs.',
  'gimlet': 'A classic famous for the story that it originated in the 19th-century British Navy, where lime juice was mixed into gin to help ward off scurvy. The crisp harmony of gin and lime makes it clean enough to work well as an aperitif.',
  'tom-collins': 'A classic made famous by an amusing 19th-century New York prank in which people were told \'Tom Collins has been talking about you\' — sending them off looking for a man who didn\'t exist. Gin, lemon, and soda water combine for a crisp, refreshing drink that\'s especially well suited to hot days.',
  'aviation': 'A classic first introduced by New York bartender Hugo Ensslin in 1916, the Aviation is said to owe its name to the pale sky-blue tint crème de violette gives it. Maraschino liqueur\'s gentle cherry note meets floral aromatics for an elegant, delicate flavor.',
  'last-word': 'A classic said to have been born at the Detroit Athletic Club in the 1920s, it was long forgotten before being rediscovered and gaining major popularity during the craft cocktail boom of the 2000s. Equal parts gin, herbal Chartreuse, maraschino, and lime combine for a flavor that\'s complex yet perfectly balanced.',
  'singapore-sling': 'A classic said to have originated at Singapore\'s iconic Raffles Hotel in the 1910s, with a story that it was designed to look like fruit juice at a time when it was difficult for women to drink in public. Layers of various liqueurs and tropical fruit give it a vibrant, exotic flavor.',
  'clover-club': 'A classic named after a gentlemen\'s club in early-20th-century Philadelphia, said to have been a favorite among the club\'s members at the time. Egg white gives it a lush foam, while raspberry-scented grenadine lends it a vivid pink color.',
  'bees-knees': 'The Bee\'s Knees is said to have emerged during Prohibition-era America in the 1920s, when honey was used to mask the rough edges of low-quality bootleg spirits. Gin\'s herbal character and honey\'s gentle sweetness combine for a smooth, easy-drinking cocktail.',
  'french-75': 'This classic is said to take its name from the formidable French 75mm field gun of World War I, the idea being that one sip hits you like a shell going off. Gin\'s herbal notes and bright lemon are softened by a gentle pour of champagne, making it a dazzling way to open a celebration.',
  'southside': 'This classic is famous for its story of being a favorite drink of gangsters from Chicago\'s South Side in the 1920s. The refreshing coolness of mint and lime meets gin for a bright, crisp sip.',
  'corpse-reviver-no-2': 'The most famous of the classic hangover-cure cocktails, it became widely known through the 1930 cocktail book of Harry Craddock, bartender at London\'s Savoy Hotel. It\'s said to be so potent that it came with the witty warning, "no more than four glasses at a time."',
  'martinez': 'Believed to have been born near San Francisco in the late 19th century, the Martinez is regarded as the direct ancestor of today\'s Dry Martini. Old Tom gin and sweet vermouth combine for a taste noticeably sweeter and richer than the modern Martini.',
  'hanky-panky': 'This classic is famous for having been created by Ada Coleman, the legendary bartender at London\'s Savoy Hotel in the early 20th century, for a regular customer. As the story goes, after his first sip he exclaimed, "By Jove! That is the real hanky-panky!" — giving the drink its name — and it\'s known for the bittersweet, minty edge of Fernet.',
  'white-lady': 'A classic that was popular in 1920s London, the White Lady can be thought of as the gin version of the Sidecar. Orange liqueur and lemon combine in a bright, elegant balance.',
  'bramble': 'A modern classic created by London bartender Dick Bradsell in the 1980s, the Bramble is said to have been inspired by the blackberry brambles of the English countryside. It\'s known for the striking way blackberry liqueur is drizzled over crushed ice, and for its bright, sweet-tart flavor.',
  'ramos-gin-fizz': 'A classic created in 1888 by New Orleans bartender Henry Ramos, famous for the legend that it must be shaken for up to twelve minutes to achieve its perfect texture. Cream, egg white, and orange blossom water come together to produce its signature rich, velvety foam.',
  'gin-fizz': 'A classic that gained great popularity in the American South from the late 19th century onward, it stands as a defining example of the Fizz-style cocktail. The lively fizz of soda combines with gin and lemon for a crisp, refreshing drink.',
  'gin-basil-smash': 'A modern classic created in 2008 by Hamburg bartender Jörg Meyer, defined by a generous amount of muddled fresh basil. Its pronounced herbal aroma gives it the freshness of sipping straight from a garden.',
  'gin-tonic': 'Said to trace back to 19th-century India, where soldiers of the British East India Company mixed the antimalarial quinine into tonic water. Adding gin to tame the bitterness gave rise to the Gin & Tonic we know today, now one of the most beloved highballs in the world.',
  'bramble-royale': 'A sparkling variation on the Bramble finished with champagne, the Bramble Royale gets a lighter, more effervescent lift from the bubbles. It suits celebrations and brunches alike.',
  'moscow-mule': 'The Moscow Mule is famously said to have been created in 1941 in Los Angeles as a joint marketing effort between a spirits distributor sitting on excess vodka and a ginger beer brand. Its signature copper mug and the punchy combination of spicy ginger beer remain hallmarks, and it\'s still one of the most popular vodka cocktails in the world.',
  'cosmopolitan': 'The Cosmopolitan is said to have been perfected by New York bartender Toby Cecchini in the 1990s, and it became a worldwide phenomenon after the cast of "Sex and the City" made it their drink of choice. The brightness of cranberry and lime plays off the vodka, giving it a taste as crisp and vibrant as its signature pink hue.',
  'bloody-mary': 'The most widely told story traces its birth to Harry\'s New York Bar in 1920s Paris, and it\'s said to have been a favorite among the writers of the era, Hemingway included. Rich tomato juice gets its kick from Worcestershire and hot sauce, making the Bloody Mary especially beloved as a brunch staple and hangover cure.',
  'white-russian': 'A cream-topped variation on the Black Russian known since the mid-20th century, though it owes its worldwide fame to 1998\'s "The Big Lebowski," where it\'s the drink of choice for the film\'s protagonist. Vodka, coffee liqueur, and cream settle into soft layers for a smooth, rich sip.',
  'black-russian': 'This cocktail is famous for the story that a bartender at a hotel in Brussels, Belgium made it in 1949 for the then-US ambassador to Belgium. It\'s a simple pairing of just vodka and coffee liqueur, but the rich, deep coffee character makes it a fitting choice for after dinner.',
  'espresso-martini': 'The story goes that legendary London bartender Dick Bradsell invented the Espresso Martini on the spot in the 1980s, after a model asked him for a drink that would "wake me up and f*** me up." With its striking espresso crema, it works equally well as the opener to an evening or a nightcap after dinner.',
  'lemon-drop-martini': 'Said to have originated at a bar in San Francisco in the 1970s, the Lemon Drop Martini is a sweet-tart, martini-style drink reminiscent of a lemon candy. Its sugared rim gives every first sip a bright, sweet-and-sour lift.',
  'vodka-gimlet': 'A reinterpretation of the Gin Gimlet made with vodka, the Vodka Gimlet lets the bright taste of lime shine without gin\'s characteristic herbal notes. It\'s known for its clean, easygoing flavor.',
  'sea-breeze': 'A cranberry-and-grapefruit highball that was popular in the United States during the 1980s, the Sea Breeze defined its era before the Cosmopolitan took over as the trend. The tartness of the two juices together makes for a crisp, refreshing summer drink.',
  'screwdriver': 'The Screwdriver is famously said to owe its name to American oil workers in the Middle East after World War II, who allegedly stirred their drinks with an actual screwdriver for lack of any other tool. Made simply from vodka and orange juice, that simplicity is exactly why it has remained a brunch favorite for so long.',
  'harvey-wallbanger': 'Said to have become popular alongside California surf culture from the 1950s through the \'70s, with a story that traces its name to a surfer. Herbal Galliano floats over orange juice, leaving a bright, sweet impression.',
  'greyhound': 'A classic enjoyed since the early 20th century, built on nothing more than grapefruit juice and vodka. A good choice when you\'re after the tart, bittersweet character of grapefruit itself.',
  'salty-dog': 'A classic variation on the Greyhound finished with a salted rim, the Salty Dog has been a favorite since the mid-20th century. The salt softens grapefruit\'s characteristic bitterness, giving the drink a well-balanced flavor.',
  'kamikaze': 'A potent sour that was popular in American club culture during the 1970s and \'80s, often enjoyed as a shot. Triple sec and lime combine for a sweet-tart flavor with a notably high kick.',
  'appletini': 'A defining example of the "-tini" cocktail trend that swept the United States in the late 1990s, the Appletini bears little resemblance to a classic Martini but won over drinkers with its bright, sweet-tart apple flavor. Its vivid green color and fruity aroma have made it a go-to easy-drinking cocktail.',
  'french-martini': 'A fruity martini-style cocktail that was popular in the US through the 1980s and \'90s, the French Martini blends raspberry liqueur with pineapple juice for a bright, sweet profile. A generous, frothy head of foam makes it just as striking to look at.',
  'chocolate-martini': 'A dessert-style cocktail that rose to popularity alongside the "-tini" cocktail boom of the 1990s and 2000s, the Chocolate Martini gets its richness from coffee liqueur and cream. It\'s rich and sweet enough to stand in for dessert.',
  'cape-codder': 'A New England classic highball said to have originated on Cape Cod, in Massachusetts, on the US East Coast. Tart-sweet cranberry juice meets vodka for a light, easy pour.',
  'vesper': 'The Vesper owes its fame to Ian Fleming\'s James Bond novel "Casino Royale," in which 007 himself orders up the recipe by name. Combining two spirits, gin and vodka, in one unusual build, it makes for a bold, distinctly dry drink.',
  'woo-woo': 'A casual highball that was popular in American bar culture during the 1980s, the Woo Woo drew attention with its provocative name. The sweetness of peach and cranberry makes it an easygoing party cocktail.',
  'sex-on-the-beach': 'Sex on the Beach is said to have been born out of a bartending competition in 1980s Florida, and its provocative name quickly made it a sensation. Peach and tropical fruit juices come together for a sweet, easygoing flavor that\'s a hit at parties.',
  'blue-lagoon': 'Said to have been created by a French bartender in the 1960s, this cocktail is instantly recognizable for its vivid blue color. Blue curaçao\'s bright orange notes and lively carbonation bring energy to a summer party.',
  'margarita': 'Its exact origin story is disputed, with several competing accounts, but it\'s believed to have begun in the Mexican border region in the 1930s–40s, and it has since become one of the most consumed cocktails in the world. The sweet-tart balance of tequila, orange liqueur, and lime, finished with a salted rim, makes for a crisp opener to any evening gathering.',
  'tommys-margarita': 'A modern classic created by bartender Julio Bermejo at Tommy\'s Mexican, the legendary San Francisco restaurant, in the 1990s. Using agave syrup in place of triple sec, it\'s regarded as a cleaner Margarita that lets tequila\'s own flavor come through more clearly.',
  'paloma': 'Its exact origin is unclear, but the Paloma is so beloved in Mexico that it\'s said to be consumed there even more than the Margarita, making it something of a national cocktail. The tang of grapefruit soda paired with tequila makes it an easy, refreshing drink to enjoy any time.',
  'tequila-sunrise': 'Popular in 1970s California, the Tequila Sunrise gained even more fame thanks to stories of the Rolling Stones enjoying it while on tour. Its name comes from the way grenadine settles toward the bottom of the glass, creating a red gradient reminiscent of a sunrise.',
  'el-diablo': 'A highball said to have first appeared in a tiki-style cocktail book published in the US in the 1940s. Tequila, cassis, and spicy ginger beer come together for a drink that\'s both fiery and refreshing — a good pick for summer.',
  'matador': 'A tequila-based sour built around pineapple, said to have originated in Mexico in the mid-20th century. It\'s known for a bright, tangy flavor.',
  'mezcal-margarita': 'A reinterpretation of the traditional Margarita using smoky mezcal, it gained major popularity from the 2010s onward alongside the craft cocktail boom. The smoky note adds a deeper, more complex layer of flavor.',
  'oaxaca-old-fashioned': 'Created in 2007 by New York bartender Phil Ward, the Oaxaca Old Fashioned is a modern classic that brings mezcal from Mexico\'s Oaxaca region into the Old Fashioned format. Agave\'s earthy, smoky character gives it a distinctive depth.',
  'ranch-water': 'Ranch Water is said to have originated in West Texas ranch culture and has surged in popularity across the United States in recent years. Made with nothing more than tequila, lime, and sparkling water, it\'s clean, refreshing, and light on calories.',
  'cantarito': 'A regional specialty of Jalisco, Mexico, traditionally served in a small clay cup. Tequila is mixed with a blend of citrus juices for a tart, refreshing drink that\'s ideal for cutting through the heat.',
  'spicy-margarita': 'A modern twist on the classic Margarita with the heat of jalapeño or cayenne pepper added in, the Spicy Margarita spread widely alongside the growth of Mexican dining culture from the 2000s onward. Its combination of spice and tang makes for a striking first sip.',
  'tequila-smash': 'A tequila-based sour made with muddled mint, the Tequila Smash is the tequila take on the Smash-style cocktail. Its refreshing mint aroma pairs well with agave\'s earthy notes.',
  'watermelon-margarita': 'A modern variation on the Margarita made with pureed watermelon, a fruit at its peak in summer, the Watermelon Margarita is especially popular as a seasonal menu item during the hot months. Watermelon\'s coolness pairs beautifully with tequila.',
  'daiquiri': 'This classic is famous for the story that it was created in the late 19th century by an American engineer working at the Daiquirí mine near Santiago de Cuba. With just three ingredients — rum, lime, and sugar — it achieves a perfect balance in its simplicity, earning it a reputation as the archetypal rum sour.',
  'mojito': 'Some trace the Mojito\'s roots back to 16th-century Cuba, but it was in early 20th-century Havana bars that it became famous in its modern form. It\'s especially well known for the story of Ernest Hemingway enjoying it at La Bodeguita del Medio in Havana, and the refreshing combination of mint and lime makes it a natural fit for hot weather or a summer gathering.',
  'cuba-libre': 'Famously said to take its name from 1898, when American soldiers celebrating Cuban independence mixed rum with the newly launched Coca-Cola and toasted "to a free Cuba." Its simplicity — just rum, cola, and lime — is likely why it has stayed beloved for so long.',
  'dark-n-stormy': 'Bermuda\'s national cocktail, named for the stormy, cloud-dark color that dark rum and ginger beer create together. Both the name and the recipe are trademarked by the rum brand Gosling\'s, making it as much a symbol of Bermuda as a drink.',
  'mai-tai': 'This classic, one of the defining drinks of tiki culture, is said to have been created in 1944 at Trader Vic\'s in Oakland, California. As the story goes, founder Victor Bergeron first served it to a Tahitian friend, who exclaimed "Maita\'i roa ae!" ("Out of this world — the best!"), giving the drink its name; two kinds of rum, orgeat, and lime come together for a deep, rich flavor.',
  'zombie': 'A legendary classic created in the 1930s by Don the Beachcomber, the founding figure of American tiki culture, the Zombie is famous for its cautionary name, warning that one drink is enough to turn you into a zombie. Multiple rums and spices build into an intense, deeply layered flavor, earning it a reputation as the pinnacle of tiki cocktails.',
  'hurricane': 'This classic was created at Pat O\'Brien\'s, the legendary New Orleans bar, in the 1940s, and is distinguished by being served in a glass shaped like a hurricane lamp. It\'s marked by a deep passion fruit character and remains an iconic drink of New Orleans\' Mardi Gras celebrations to this day.',
  'painkiller': 'This classic was created at the legendary Soggy Dollar Bar in the British Virgin Islands in the 1970s or \'80s and remains one of the defining cocktails of the region to this day. Coconut cream, pineapple, and orange blend richly into a flavor that lives up to its name by making your worries disappear.',
  'jungle-bird': 'This classic is said to have been created in 1978 at the Hilton hotel in Kuala Lumpur, Malaysia, and stands out among tiki cocktails for its rare use of bittersweet Campari. The distinctive balance between pineapple\'s sweetness and Campari\'s bitterness has driven a major resurgence in its popularity in recent years.',
  'planters-punch': 'This storied rum punch is said to have originated on 19th-century Caribbean plantations, combining dark rum with a rich mix of tropical fruit juices. It stands as one of the defining classics of Caribbean rum culture.',
  'pi-a-colada': 'This iconic classic was created in 1954 at the Caribe Hilton hotel in San Juan, Puerto Rico, and went on to be named Puerto Rico\'s official national cocktail. Blended coconut cream and pineapple give it a lush texture that instantly conjures a tropical getaway.',
  'el-presidente': 'A classic that was all the rage in 1920s Havana, El Presidente is said to have been named in honor of Cuba\'s president at the time. White rum and vermouth come together for an elegant, refined character.',
  'hemingway-daiquiri': 'Named for the story that Ernest Hemingway, unable to have sugar due to his diabetes, favored his Daiquiri without it, this classic originated at the El Floridita bar in Havana, Cuba. Grapefruit\'s bitterness and maraschino\'s subtle cherry note make it drier and more complex than the original Daiquiri.',
  'corn-n-oil': 'This classic hails from Barbados in the Caribbean; the origin of its unusual name isn\'t entirely clear, but it has long been a local favorite. Dark rum and falernum come together for a richly spiced, sweet character.',
  'three-dots-and-a-dash': 'Believed to have originated at Trader Vic\'s in the 1930s, this classic takes its name from the Morse code signal for "V," for victory, a nod to the World War II era it emerged from. Layer upon layer of ingredients give it a showy, complex character.',
  'rum-punch': 'A popular style of rum punch enjoyed across the Caribbean for generations, with recipes that vary slightly from island to island. Rum and tropical fruit juices come together for an easygoing party cocktail.',
  'caipirinha': 'Brazil\'s national cocktail, made in the traditional way by muddling lime with cachaça, the country\'s sugarcane spirit, a method that has endured for centuries. Its bright, bold flavor makes it especially refreshing in hot weather.',
  'old-cuban': 'A modern classic created in 2001 by New York bartender Audrey Saunders, this drink reads like a cross between a mojito and a champagne cocktail. The cool snap of mint meets champagne\'s lively fizz for a finish that\'s both elegant and refreshing.',
  'sidecar': 'A classic whose birthplace is disputed between 1920s Paris and London, with one story claiming it was created for an American military officer who rode around in a motorcycle sidecar during World War I. Served with a sugared rim, the interplay of cognac, orange liqueur, and lemon gives it an elegant, sophisticated flavor.',
  'brandy-alexander': 'This drink is said to have first appeared in Britain in the late 19th century, originally made with gin before the brandy version went on to become more popular. Chocolate liqueur and cream come together for a dessert-like sweetness, making it a fitting nightcap to close out a meal.',
  'french-connection': 'This cocktail is said to have caught on alongside the popularity of the film of the same name, released in 1971. Made from just two ingredients, Cognac and amaretto, its sweet almond notes gently wrap around the brandy\'s weight.',
  'stinger': 'This classic was popular in American high society from the late 19th into the early 20th century, standing as something of a symbol of Jazz Age New York nightlife. Cognac meets mint liqueur for a cool, clean finish that makes it a good choice after a meal.',
  'corpse-reviver-no-1': 'True to its name as a hangover cure meant to "revive the dead," this recipe has been handed down from British hotel bars from the late 19th into the early 20th century. Built around Cognac with sweet vermouth and apple brandy, it leaves a warm, substantial impression.',
  'pisco-sour': 'Peru\'s national cocktail, said to have been created in the early 20th century by an American bartender in Lima using pisco, Peru\'s native grape brandy. It\'s recognized by its rich egg-white foam and a few drops of bitters as garnish, and both Peru and Chile claim it as their own with equal pride.',
  'between-the-sheets': 'Said to have been popular in Paris during the 1920s and \'30s, Between the Sheets is also well known for its suggestive name. Combining two spirits, cognac and rum, it delivers a bolder, more complex flavor than the Sidecar.',
  'jack-rose': 'A classic that saw great popularity in the early-20th-century United States, defined by the distinctive apple character of applejack brandy. Its name is said to come from the rosy hue that grenadine\'s subtle sweetness gives it.',
  'americano': 'This cocktail is said to have first been made in Milan, Italy in the 1860s, originally called the "Milano-Torino" after the bartender who created it. It\'s said to have later picked up the name "Americano" as it grew especially popular with American tourists. Campari\'s bitterness and sweet vermouth\'s gentle sweetness are softened by soda water into a low-proof but unmistakably present aperitif, making it a great choice before a meal.',
  'aperol-spritz': 'A defining cocktail that originated in Italy\'s Veneto region in the early 20th century and drove the worldwide aperitivo boom of the 2010s onward. Aperol\'s gentle bitterness and orange aroma meet prosecco for a bright orange hue, making it a wonderful light aperitif to kick off an evening.',
  'kir-royale': 'An elevated version of the Kir made with champagne instead of white wine, this drink lets the rich berry flavor of cassis liqueur bloom beautifully alongside the bubbles. It makes a fitting aperitif to open a celebration or a special evening.',
  'bellini': 'A classic born at Harry\'s Bar, the legendary bar in Venice, Italy, in the 1930s and 40s, said to have been named for the colors found in Renaissance painter Giovanni Bellini\'s paintings. The soft sweetness of peach puree meets prosecco, adding a touch of brightness to any brunch.',
  'mimosa': 'This classic is said to have originated at the Ritz Hotel in Paris in the 1920s, taking its name from its resemblance to the color of yellow mimosa flowers. Its simple formula of equal parts orange juice and champagne has made it an iconic fixture of the brunch table.',
  'grasshopper': 'This drink is said to have first been created in 1918 by a bartender in New Orleans for a cocktail competition. Its rich sweetness, reminiscent of mint chocolate chip ice cream, makes it an easy stand-in for dessert.',
  'mudslide': 'This cocktail is said to have been born at a beach bar in the Cayman Islands in the Caribbean in the 1970s. Vodka, coffee liqueur, and Irish cream are blended together into something as smooth and sweet as a milkshake, making it an easy substitute for dessert.',
  'long-island-iced-tea': 'Said to have been invented by a bartender on Long Island, New York, in the 1970s. Five different spirits go into the glass, yet the splash of cola gives it the look of actual iced tea; it\'s surprisingly strong for how smoothly it goes down, which has made it a longtime party favorite.',
  'amaretto-sour': 'A classic sour that was hugely popular in the US in the 1970s, the Amaretto Sour pairs almond-scented amaretto with bright lemon for a sweet yet balanced flavor. Easy to drink, it has kept a steady following ever since.',
  'godfather': 'This cocktail is said to have taken its name from the hit film of the same title in the 1970s. Scotch whisky is joined by amaretto\'s almond sweetness for a drink that\'s weighty yet leaves a sweet finish, making it a good choice for a nightcap.',
  'b-52': 'This layered shot is said to have first appeared in Canada in the 1970s, and the most popular theory is that it was named after the B-52 bomber. Coffee liqueur, Irish cream, and triple sec are stacked by density into distinct layers, and the striking visual always draws attention at a party.',
  'godmother': 'Known as the vodka counterpart to the Godfather, this drink is said to have appeared on menus around the same time. Clean vodka is softened by amaretto\'s almond aroma for an easy, smooth sip.',
  'vodka-martini': 'The vodka take on the Dry Martini, famous for the line "shaken, not stirred." Cleaner and more neutral in flavor than its gin counterpart, it\'s the classic variation people reach for according to taste.',
  'dirty-martini': 'A variation on the Dry Martini enjoyed since the early-to-mid 20th century, the Dirty Martini adds a splash of olive brine for a savory, salty edge. That umami note layered over gin\'s clean bite makes it a natural stand-in for an appetizer.',
  'espresso-old-fashioned': 'A modern twist on the classic Old Fashioned, the Espresso Old Fashioned adds cold brew coffee, making it a great choice when you want coffee and whiskey together. Its rich, bittersweet profile also makes a fine after-dinner nightcap.',
  'negroni-sbagliato': 'This drink is famous for the story of its accidental creation in 1970s Milan, when a bartender reached for sparkling wine instead of gin by mistake ("sbagliato" is Italian for "mistaken"). It keeps the Negroni\'s bittersweet balance while adding a lighter, livelier sparkle.',
  'manhattan-perfect': 'This variation splits the Manhattan\'s vermouth evenly between dry and sweet, toning down the sweetness for a more balanced style. True to its name, the Perfect Manhattan is prized for its precise, refined balance.',
  'whiskey-collins': 'A whiskey-based reinterpretation of the Tom Collins, the Whiskey Collins pairs bourbon\'s sweet oak notes with bright lemon. It\'s a refreshing highball that still lets the whiskey\'s presence come through.',
  'irish-mule': 'A reinterpretation of the Moscow Mule with Irish whiskey standing in for vodka, giving it a softer, more mellow character. Spicy ginger beer and lime bring it to a refreshing finish.',
  'kentucky-mule': 'A Southern American take on the Moscow Mule that swaps in bourbon, spreading widely from the 2010s on with the craft cocktail boom. Bourbon\'s understated sweetness pairs nicely with the bite of ginger beer.',
  'dark-stormy-rye': 'A modern twist that reimagines the classic Dark \'n\' Stormy with rye whiskey. Rye\'s characteristic spice plays well against the bite of ginger beer for a refreshing result.',
  'cherry-blossom': 'A classic introduced in an American cocktail book in the early 20th century, known for its vivid pink hue and cherry aroma. Built on a brandy base, it offers a sweet, smooth flavor.',
  'americano-spritz': 'A sparkling take on the Americano made with prosecco, the Americano Spritz is drier and more bitter than an Aperol Spritz. It\'s a great choice when you want to savor Campari\'s deep bitterness in full.',
  'rum-old-fashioned': 'This variation reimagines the Old Fashioned with dark rum in place of whiskey, letting you enjoy rum\'s own rich, robust character within the classic formula. The classic method of preparation stays exactly the same.',
  'bourbon-renewal': 'A modern classic that emerged in the 2000s, the Bourbon Renewal brings deep fruit character to a bourbon sour with the addition of blackcurrant liqueur. It\'s a classic build given a contemporary twist.',
  'naked-and-famous': 'A modern classic created in 2011 by New York bartender Joaquín Simó, it reinterprets the structure of the Paper Plane using mezcal and Aperol in place of the original spirits. Its smoky, bittersweet complexity has made it a favorite in craft cocktail bars.',
  'brooklyn': 'Named for the borough across the river from Manhattan, the Brooklyn was popular in New York in the early 20th century but faded from memory for a time once its key ingredient, Amer Picon, became hard to find; it has since been rediscovered. Maraschino and amaro combine for a drier, more complex flavor than the Manhattan.',
  'pink-lady': 'A classic that gained popularity during Prohibition-era America in the early 20th century, the Pink Lady is known for its bright pink hue and soft, airy foam. Apple brandy and grenadine combine for a sweet, elegant flavor.',
  'tuxedo': 'A classic said to take its name from the Tuxedo Park Country Club near New York in the late 19th century. Similar to a Martini but with a subtle sweetness from maraschino liqueur, it\'s a touch softer and more refined.',
  'frozen-daiquiri': 'A chilled, blended take on the daiquiri, this version is also well known as a favorite of Ernest Hemingway\'s. It\'s a classic best enjoyed ice-cold on a hot summer day.',
  'frozen-margarita': 'A summer take on the traditional Margarita, blended with ice into a slush, it became so popular in 1970s Texas that it even inspired the invention of dedicated frozen-drink machines. Cold and refreshing, it\'s especially suited to hot summer days.',
  'whiskey-ginger': 'Made from nothing more than whiskey and ginger ale, the Whiskey Ginger is one of the most popular highballs found in bars around the world. It\'s an unpretentious combination that anyone can enjoy without a second thought.',
  'rum-and-coke': 'Essentially a brand-agnostic, casual take on the Cuba Libre, Rum and Coke has been one of the most commonly ordered drink combinations in bars around the world throughout the 20th century. It\'s simple, but it\'s a combination that never fails, making it an easy, reliable choice.',
  'vodka-soda': 'One of the simplest highballs there is, the Vodka Soda has remained a steady favorite thanks to its low calorie count, making it an easy choice even while watching what you drink. It lets vodka\'s own clean character come through untouched.',
  'gin-rickey': 'A classic said to be named after Colonel Joe Rickey, a Washington, D.C. lobbyist, in the late 19th century. With no sugar added, it\'s dry and crisp — a perfect thirst-quencher on a hot summer day.',
  'algonquin': 'A classic named after New York\'s famed Algonquin Hotel in the early 20th century, when the hotel was also known as a gathering place for literary figures. Pineapple juice\'s gentle sweetness meets the spice of rye whiskey for a well-balanced drink.',
  'black-manhattan': 'The Black Manhattan swaps the traditional Manhattan\'s sweet vermouth for bittersweet amaro, a modern twist that caught on in craft cocktail bars from the 2000s onward. The result is a deeper, more bittersweet drink especially beloved among whiskey lovers.',
  'seelbach': 'This drink is said to have been created in 1917 at the Seelbach Hotel in Louisville, Kentucky, though it was then forgotten for decades before being rediscovered and popularized again in the 1990s — giving it one of the more interesting backstories among the classics. Bourbon\'s weight, joined by two kinds of bitters, adds deep flavor to champagne for a celebratory pour.',
  'scofflaw': 'This classic takes its name from "scofflaw," a term coined during Prohibition-era America in the 1920s for someone who flouted the law to drink in secret. Rye whiskey and grenadine combine for a flavor that\'s both spicy and refreshingly bright.',
  'kentucky-buck': 'A bourbon-based highball said to have originated in Kentucky in the 19th century, one branch of the broader family of Buck-style cocktails. Bourbon\'s sweet oak notes meet spicy ginger beer for a comfortable, easy-drinking pour.',
  'de-la-louisiane': 'Named after La Louisiane, a storied restaurant in New Orleans, this classic represents one of the signature styles of New Orleans cocktail culture. Benedictine\'s herbal, honeyed notes meet rye whiskey for a deep, elegant flavor.',
  'whiskey-daisy': 'The whiskey take on the 19th-century Daisy-style cocktail, the Whiskey Daisy is known for grenadine\'s subtle sweetness and soda water\'s refreshing fizz. It\'s a classic that\'s easy to enjoy without being fussy.',
  'horses-neck': 'A venerable highball that first appeared in the US in the late 19th century, named for its garnish — a long spiral of lemon peel draped over the rim like a horse\'s neck. The pairing of whiskey and ginger ale is clean and unassuming, easy for anyone to enjoy.',
  'presbyterian': 'A classic highball that emerged in the United States in the late 19th century, the Presbyterian mixes ginger ale and soda water in equal parts to mellow out whiskey\'s flavor. It\'s a clean, easygoing cocktail that\'s simple to enjoy anytime.',
  'improved-whiskey-cocktail': 'Considered a forerunner to the Old Fashioned dating back even further into the 19th century, the Improved Whiskey Cocktail earned its name from the maraschino liqueur added to "improve" the original whiskey cocktail. It\'s a fascinating step back into the early history of whiskey cocktails.',
  'remember-the-maine': 'Named to commemorate the sinking of the USS Maine in 1898, the event that sparked the Spanish-American War, this classic became known through 1930s cocktail books. A touch of cherry liqueur\'s gentle sweetness and a dash of absinthe give it its distinctive flavor.',
  'frisco-sour': 'A classic sour combining rye whiskey and Bénédictine, said to have originated on the American West Coast in the early 20th century. Herbal, honeyed notes wrap gently around the whiskey\'s spice.',
  'suburban': 'Recorded in American cocktail books in the late 19th century, the Suburban brings together the unusual combination of rye whiskey, rum, and port wine. The result is a rich, complex flavor.',
  'toronto': 'Featured in Canadian cocktail books in the early 20th century, the Toronto can be thought of as a Canadian take on the Manhattan. Fernet\'s bittersweet, minty edge meets rye whiskey for a bold, distinctive flavor.',
  'waldorf-cocktail': 'Recorded in American cocktail books in the early 20th century, the Waldorf Cocktail is defined by equal parts rye whiskey and vermouth with a dash of absinthe. It\'s a simple build with a bold, intense flavor.',
  'trinidad-sour': 'A bold modern classic created in 2010 by New York bartender Giuseppe Gonzalez, the Trinidad Sour sent a jolt through the industry by making Angostura bitters — normally used only in small dashes — its main ingredient. Its deep spice notes and almond-scented orgeat syrup combine for a distinctive, complex flavor.',
  'kentucky-colonel': 'Named for Kentucky, the home of bourbon, the Kentucky Colonel pairs Benedictine\'s herbal notes with bourbon\'s oaky depth. It\'s a simple build that delivers a surprisingly rich flavor.',
  'whiskey-rickey': 'A classic whiskey-based reinterpretation of the Gin Rickey, the Whiskey Rickey is enjoyed dry, with no added sugar, making it a great choice when you want the whiskey\'s own character to shine through.',
  'blinker': 'A classic that appeared in Prohibition-era American cocktail books of the 1920s, the Blinker stands out for its unusual use of grapefruit juice. Rye whiskey\'s spice meets grapefruit\'s bitterness for a memorable flavor.',
  'fancy-free': 'Recorded in American cocktail books in the early 20th century, the Fancy Free is a bourbon cocktail that lives up to its name with a light, easygoing character. Maraschino\'s gentle sweetness balances against two kinds of bitters.',
  'twentieth-century': 'This classic takes its name from the "20th Century Limited," the legendary luxury train that connected New York and Chicago in the 1930s. The subtle chocolate notes of white crème de cacao meet gin for an elegant, sophisticated flavor.',
  'white-negroni': 'A modern classic said to have been improvised by a French bartender in 2001, swapping Campari for French Suze to give the drink a clear, pale color. The herbal notes come through more brightly, while the Negroni\'s signature bittersweet balance stays intact.',
  'pegu-club': 'This classic takes its name from the Pegu Club, a social club in colonial-era Yangon, Myanmar, in the early 20th century. Orange liqueur and bitters wrap gently around the gin\'s herbal notes, giving the drink a crisp, refreshing character.',
  'alaska-cocktail': 'A classic first introduced in an early 20th-century American cocktail book, the Alaska Cocktail is said to have, despite its name, no direct connection to Alaska. Gin combines with the herbal intensity of Chartreuse for a distinctive, richly flavored drink.',
  'bijou': 'True to its name, French for "jewel," the Bijou is a late-19th-century classic said to liken its gin, vermouth, and Chartreuse to a ruby, an emerald, and a sapphire respectively. Layers of herbal notes and sweetness make it a great choice when you\'re in the mood for something rich and complex.',
  'monkey-gland': 'A classic created in the 1920s by Harry MacElhone, bartender at the Ritz Hotel in Paris, said to be named after a controversial rejuvenation procedure that was making headlines at the time. The bright sweetness of orange juice and grenadine gets a subtle lift from a dash of absinthe.',
  'bronx': 'A classic that was just as popular as the Manhattan in early 20th-century New York, the Bronx is said to be named after the then newly opened Bronx Zoo. The addition of fresh orange juice gives it a notably bright, vivid character among Martini-style cocktails.',
  'gin-daisy': 'One branch of the Daisy-style cocktails that have been made since the late 19th century, marked by the subtle sweetness of grenadine. A splash of soda water finishes it off with a light, refreshing touch.',
  'breakfast-martini': 'A modern classic created in 1990s London by legendary bartender Salvatore Calabrese, inspired by his own breakfast of toast and marmalade. The sweet-tart brightness of orange marmalade meets gin for a drink that opens up a brunch beautifully.',
  'blue-moon': 'A classic popular in the early 20th-century United States, the Blue Moon is distinguished by the soft violet hue of crème de violette. Its lavender-like aroma paired with bright lemon gives it a distinctive charm.',
  'army-navy': 'A classic said to have originated in American military clubs in the early 20th century, the Army & Navy is known for the nutty almond notes of orgeat syrup. Combined with gin\'s herbal character, it makes for a subtle, elegant drink.',
  'gin-sour': 'Essentially the gin counterpart to the Whiskey Sour, this classic takes on a smoother, silkier texture when egg white is added. It lets gin\'s own herbal character come through crisp and clean.',
  'fifty-fifty-martini': 'A softer take on the Dry Martini with the vermouth ratio raised considerably, the Fifty-Fifty Martini has been finding new favor in craft cocktail bars in recent years. The gin\'s intensity is tempered, making it an easy, approachable pour.',
  'satans-whiskers': 'Said to have been created at a London hotel bar in the 1930s, Satan\'s Whiskers belies its devilish name with a surprisingly balanced, elegant flavor. Its many ingredients layer together for a smooth complexity.',
  'gin-gin-mule': 'A modern classic created by celebrated New York bartender Audrey Saunders in the early 2000s, blending the muddled mint and lime of a Mojito with the structure of a Moscow Mule. The spice of ginger beer and the coolness of mint make it especially suited to summer.',
  'cardinale': 'Something of a dry cousin to the Negroni, the Cardinale uses dry vermouth in place of sweet vermouth, making it crisper and letting gin\'s character stand out more. It finishes bitter yet clean.',
  'saturn': 'This classic was created in 1967 by American bartender J. "Popo" Galsini and made its name by winning an international cocktail competition of the time. Passion fruit and orgeat combine for a subtle, distinctive tiki flavor.',
  'journalist': 'A classic recorded in American cocktail books in the early 20th century, the Journalist owes its balanced character to a fairly elaborate list of ingredients. Two vermouths meet citrus for a deep, multilayered flavor.',
  'silver-fizz': 'A variation on the Gin Fizz made with the addition of egg white, giving it an even smoother, richer foam. It stands as one of the classic fizz style\'s most popular variations.',
  'diamond-fizz': 'A glamorous take on the gin fizz with champagne standing in for soda water, this drink has been regarded as a cocktail for special occasions since the early 20th century. Its fine, delicate bubbles lend an extra touch of luxury.',
  'vodka-collins': 'A vodka-based reinterpretation of the Tom Collins, the Vodka Collins swaps gin\'s herbal character for vodka\'s clean neutrality. Bright lemon and soda bring it to a refreshing finish.',
  'caipiroska': 'A vodka-based twist on the Caipirinha, it spread and gained popularity in regions where cachaça was hard to find. The tart brightness of lime and sugar remains fully intact.',
  'bay-breeze': 'A tropical-juice highball that had its moment in the US in the 1980s, built around the pairing of cranberry and pineapple juice. Sweet and easygoing, it suits outdoor get-togethers and summer parties well.',
  'madras': 'A tart-sweet highball mixing cranberry and orange juice, popular in the US through the 1970s and \'80s. Light and easy to drink, it suits casual daytime gatherings just as well as evening ones.',
  'flirtini': 'A modern cocktail that became widely known after Samantha, a character on the 2000s American show "Sex and the City," was shown enjoying it, the Flirtini is distinguished by being served in a martini glass. The sweet fruitiness of vodka and pineapple juice meets champagne\'s lively bubbles for a bright, festive drink.',
  'balalaika': 'Named after the traditional Russian stringed instrument, the Balalaika reimagines the Sidecar with vodka in place of brandy. Orange liqueur and lemon\'s brightness come together for a well-balanced drink.',
  'watermelon-martini': 'A relatively modern cocktail built around watermelon, a fruit at its peak in summer, the Watermelon Martini has become a regular on seasonal summer menus since the 2000s. Its fresh, sweet watermelon aroma spreads brightly, making it especially fitting for hot weather.',
  'cucumber-mint-vodka-smash': 'A modern-style cocktail built by muddling cucumber and mint, it has grown popular in recent years alongside the craft cocktail trend of using fresh vegetables. Its aroma is crisp and garden-fresh.',
  'vodka-sunrise': 'A vodka-based reinterpretation of the Tequila Sunrise, the Vodka Sunrise keeps the same red gradient created by orange juice and grenadine. It\'s smooth and sweet, making it easy to enjoy without much fuss.',
  'white-spider': 'A classic from the same family as the Stinger, using vodka in place of cognac for a cleaner, crisper result. A cool wash of mint makes it a good choice for cleansing the palate after a meal.',
  'chi-chi': 'A reinterpretation of the piña colada with vodka in place of rum, this variation became popular at American resorts in the 1970s and \'80s among drinkers who preferred vodka to rum. It keeps all the coconut and pineapple tropical flavor intact.',
  'melon-ball': 'A melon liqueur-based highball that was popular in the US in the 1980s, instantly recognizable by its bright green color. Sweet melon and pineapple combine to capture the mood of a summer party.',
  'bloody-maria': 'This variation swaps the vodka in the well-known Bloody Mary for tequila, and it took hold in the late 20th-century United States as brunch culture spread. Agave\'s distinct earthiness pairs well with the spicy tomato and seasonings, making it a natural fit for a hangover cure or a brunch table.',
  'salty-chihuahua': 'A tequila-based reinterpretation of the Greyhound, the Salty Chihuahua uses a salted rim to bring out the bitterness of grapefruit even more. It\'s known for its bright, unfussy flavor.',
  'mexican-mule': 'A tequila-based reinterpretation of the Moscow Mule that spread with the craft cocktail boom. Agave\'s earthy character meets spicy ginger beer for an unusual but appealing combination.',
  'batanga': 'Best known for the story that in 1961 a bar owner in Jalisco, Mexico stirred together cola and tequila with the blade of a knife. A salt rim adds a savory edge, making it an unfussy, refreshing highball.',
  'rosita': 'This cocktail reinterprets the Negroni family of classics with tequila, and it\'s said to have first been introduced in an American cocktail book in the late 20th century. Tequila\'s agave character meets Campari\'s bitterness for a rich, complex flavor.',
  'tequila-old-fashioned': 'This variation reworks the Old Fashioned with tequila, and has gained popularity in recent years alongside the craft cocktail boom. Agave syrup and the oaky notes of reposado combine for a deep, layered flavor.',
  'mexican-75': 'A modern reinterpretation of the French 75 with tequila in place of gin, this variation trades gin\'s herbal notes for the distinctive character of agave. Bright lime and champagne\'s effervescence come together in easy harmony.',
  'chapala': 'A classic named after a well-known lake region in Mexico, this is a bright, tequila-based sour. The interplay of orange and lime makes it a refreshingly crisp pour.',
  'division-bell': 'A modern classic created in 2009 by New York bartender Phil Ward, it pairs the smokiness of mezcal with the bittersweet character of Aperol in a distinctive combination. It is a style especially beloved in craft cocktail bars.',
  'mezcal-negroni': 'This modern twist swaps the traditional Negroni\'s gin for smoky mezcal, and it spread widely from the 2010s onward alongside the craft cocktail boom. Mezcal\'s smokiness meets Campari\'s bitterness for an even deeper impression.',
  'mexican-firing-squad': 'A classic said to have originated at a bar in Mexico City in the 1930s, it belies its provocative name with a well-balanced, tangy flavor. A subtle bitterness from Angostura bitters lingers in the background.',
  'tequila-slammer': 'A party shot that took off alongside club culture in the 1980s and \'90s. Tequila and lemon-lime soda are poured into a glass, slammed on the bar or with a palm to make it fizz, and downed in one go — the ritual is half the fun.',
  'freddy-fudpucker': 'A Screwdriver topped with a float of Galliano, popular in American party culture of the 1970s. Bright orange juice carries a whisper of herbal Galliano on top, making for easy, breezy drinking.',
  'brave-bull': 'This cocktail is said to have been created in Mexico in the 1970s, and it\'s essentially a tequila-based take on the Black Russian. Agave\'s earthy character meets coffee\'s bitterness for a rich drink that makes for a satisfying finish after dinner.',
  'queens-park-swizzle': 'This classic is said to have been created in the early 20th century at the Queen\'s Park Hotel in Trinidad, and is distinguished by its swizzling technique — stirring crushed ice with a swizzle stick until frost forms on the glass. A generous amount of muddled mint gives it a cool, refreshing lift.',
  'navy-grog': 'This classic was created at Trader Vic\'s in the 1940s and is said to have drawn inspiration from an actual naval rum ration recipe. Three kinds of rum come together in balance with honey and citrus.',
  'fog-cutter': 'This classic was created at Trader Vic\'s, the legendary American tiki bar, sometime in the 1930s or \'40s, and is said to take its name from the idea that it\'s strong enough to cut through fog. It combines three spirits at once — rum, brandy, and gin — yet goes down surprisingly smoothly thanks to orange and orgeat.',
  'scorpion-bowl': 'The Scorpion Bowl reimagines a classic tiki drink traditionally shared over a big communal bowl, scaled down here to a single serving. Orange and orgeat come together for a soft, vibrant flavor.',
  'beachcomber': 'A classic sour said to have been created in 1930s Hollywood by Donn Beach, the bartender credited with founding tiki culture. Rum is layered with orange liqueur and maraschino liqueur, giving it a bright yet well-balanced flavor.',
  'bacardi-cocktail': 'A variation on the Daiquiri with grenadine added, the Bacardi Cocktail was once so well known that a US court ruling held it could only be made with Bacardi-brand rum. It\'s marked by a soft pink hue and a bright lime aroma.',
  'barbados-rum-punch': 'This classic follows a traditional Caribbean punch formula handed down through generations: "one of sour, two of sweet, three of strong, four of weak." Rum\'s deep flavor and lime\'s brightness strike a satisfying balance.',
  'rum-swizzle': 'A traditional cocktail said to hail from Bermuda in the Caribbean, the Rum Swizzle is known for its frosty texture, created by stirring crushed ice briskly with a swizzle stick. The crisp brightness of rum and lime gives it an unmistakable tropical-getaway feel.',
  'kingston-negroni': 'This modern classic reimagines the Negroni with the funky character of Jamaican rum, and it made its name as it caught on among New York bartenders in the 2010s. Campari\'s bitterness meets the rum\'s weight for a drink that holds up well as an aperitif.',
  'bushwacker': 'A milkshake-style tiki dessert cocktail that originated at a beach bar on Florida\'s Gulf Coast in the 1970s, it layers coconut, coffee liqueur, and cream into a rich blend that makes a fine stand-in for dessert.',
  'rum-runner': 'This classic is said to have originated at a tiki bar in Florida\'s Florida Keys in the 1950s or \'60s, taking its name from the Prohibition-era smugglers known as "rum runners." Black currant and banana liqueurs add a rich, showy tropical fruit character.',
  'bahama-mama': 'A tropical-fruit-forward tiki cocktail that rose to popularity alongside Caribbean resort culture in the 1970s and \'80s, it blends two kinds of rum with pineapple and orange for a drink that practically smells like vacation.',
  'missionarys-downfall': 'This classic appeared in cocktail books from the early days of American tiki culture in the 1930s, distinguished by its unusual method of blending fresh peach and mint. It\'s a soft, fruit-forward summer cocktail.',
  'test-pilot': 'Said to have been created at Trader Vic\'s in 1941, the Test Pilot is a true tiki classic built from layers of rum and falernum that stack into a complex, well-rounded flavor. It\'s often counted among the defining drinks of tiki\'s golden age.',
  'doctor-funk': 'A tiki cocktail said to be named after a doctor who really did practice in Samoa, in the South Pacific, in the early 20th century. Tart-sweet lime and grenadine are lifted by a dash of absinthe, whose subtle herbal note lends an exotic air.',
  'bees-kiss': 'This short, strong classic even made it into cocktail books from the Prohibition era in the early 20th-century United States. White rum is rounded out with honey and cream for a smooth, sweet finish, making it a lovely light pour after a meal.',
  'xyz-cocktail': 'A rum reinterpretation of the White Lady, the XYZ Cocktail is said to have been named for the last letters of the alphabet, meaning there was nothing left to improve upon. It\'s simple but remarkably well balanced.',
  'rum-sour': 'A classic rum-based sour, the Rum Sour is a heavier, silkier take on the Daiquiri, with egg white lending it a smooth texture. It\'s a great choice when you want the rum\'s own character to shine through.',
  'ti-punch': 'An everyday national drink of the French Caribbean islands of Martinique and Guadeloupe; the name itself is French for "little punch." It\'s built from just cane rum, lime, and sugar, but local tradition calls for each drinker to adjust the ratio to their own taste rather than follow a fixed recipe.',
  'hotel-nacional': 'A classic named after Havana\'s iconic Hotel Nacional, said to have originated at the hotel\'s bar in the early 20th century. Apricot brandy and pineapple combine for a bright, exotic flavor.',
  'mary-pickford': 'A classic named after a real Hollywood silent-film star who spent time in Havana, Cuba, in the 1920s. Marked by the bright sweetness of pineapple and grenadine, it\'s said to have been popular among the Hollywood stars who visited Havana at the time.',
  'cable-car': 'A modern classic created by a San Francisco bartender in the 1990s, named after the city\'s iconic cable cars. Its cinnamon-sugar rim plays beautifully off the spice notes of the spiced rum.',
  'jet-pilot': 'Born at the height of American tiki culture in the 1950s, this classic is an intense blend of multiple rums, absinthe, and citrus. Tiki enthusiasts count it among the standout classics of the genre.',
  'brandy-crusta': 'A classic said to have originated in 19th-century New Orleans, the Brandy Crusta is regarded as the forerunner of many later sour cocktails, the Sidecar among them. Its sugared rim sets off the weight of the brandy and the brightness of citrus.',
  'champs-lys-es': 'A classic named after Paris\'s iconic avenue, the Champs-Élysées, first introduced in a French cocktail book in the early 20th century. The herbal notes of Chartreuse mingle with cognac for an elegant, complex flavor.',
  'brandy-sour': 'The simplest form of a brandy-based sour, this classic has long been enjoyed steadily across various European countries. It lets brandy\'s own character shine through in a refreshingly simple form.',
  'japanese-cocktail': 'A venerable classic that even appears in the first American cocktail book, published in the 1860s, it\'s said to have been named in honor of a Japanese delegation visiting the United States at the time. Cognac paired with almond-scented orgeat syrup gives it a subtle, refined flavor.',
  'applejack-rabbit': 'A classic sour built on applejack brandy, once produced in New Jersey in the early 20th century. Maple syrup\'s gentle sweetness pairs with the apple brandy, making it especially fitting for cooler weather.',
  'b-b': 'The pairing of brandy and Bénédictine had long been enjoyed in France\'s Cognac region, and a New York bartender is said to have popularized it in the early 20th century by putting it on the menu simply as "B&B." With just two ingredients, it still delivers layers of flavor as Cognac\'s weight meets Bénédictine\'s herbal, honeyed notes, making it a digestif that really shows its worth after a meal.',
  'metropolitan': 'A brandy sour built with blackcurrant liqueur, marked by a rich berry aroma. It\'s a variation that adds deep fruit notes to the classic sour structure.',
  'east-india-cocktail': 'This classic was first recorded in an American cocktail book in the late 19th century, and its name appears to draw on the era\'s fascination with the East India trade and the exotic image of pineapple. Built around Cognac with subtle notes of pineapple and orange, it also works nicely as an aperitif.',
  'death-in-the-afternoon': 'This recipe is said to have been devised by Ernest Hemingway himself, and it takes its name from the title of one of his novels. Hemingway reportedly wrote, half-jokingly, that it should be sipped "three to five of them slowly," a hint at just how strong this cocktail really is.',
  'champagne-cocktail': 'One of the oldest champagne cocktails, dating back to the 19th century, the Champagne Cocktail is known for its striking presentation as a bitters-soaked sugar cube slowly dissolves in the glass. It\'s an elegant way to open a celebration or a special evening.',
  'poinsettia': 'A frequent fixture on holiday-season menus, this variation on the Mimosa takes its name from the poinsettia, the flower long associated with Christmas. Cranberry\'s deep red hue lends it an extra festive touch.',
  'bicicletta': 'A simpler style of spritz enjoyed in northern Italy even before the Aperol Spritz, the Bicicletta is said to take its name from being light enough to sip while riding a bicycle and still keep a pleasant buzz. Campari\'s bitterness stands out more here, making for a drier aperitivo.',
  'hugo-spritz': 'A relatively recent creation from the early 2000s, this drink was invented by bartender Roland Gruber in South Tyrol, Italy, and quickly gained popularity as an alternative to the Aperol Spritz. The subtle floral notes of elderflower liqueur give it a bright, refreshing character.',
  'pimms-cup': 'The Pimm\'s Cup traces back to a herbal liqueur created in mid-19th-century London by the owner of an oyster restaurant, meant as a digestive aid, and it has since become an iconic drink of British summer garden parties and Wimbledon. Cucumber and mint give it a crisp, quintessentially English summer character.',
  'sherry-cobbler': 'One of the original cocktails to make use of crushed ice, the Sherry Cobbler was hugely popular in 19th-century America. It was also known for being served with a straw, a novelty at the time, and sherry\'s gentle sweetness paired with fresh orange makes for an easy, light drink.',
  'adonis': 'The Adonis is a classic said to have been created at a New York hotel bar to celebrate the hit Broadway musical of the same name, which was a sensation in the late 19th century. Sherry\'s gentle sweetness meets vermouth for a low-proof drink with surprising depth of flavor.',
  'vermouth-spritz': 'Riding the recent wave of interest in lower-alcohol aperitifs, this style lets vermouth\'s subtle, nuanced flavors shine through in a lighter format. It makes an easygoing pre-dinner drink you can sip on for a while.',
  'port-flip': 'This drink belongs to the "flip" style of cocktails that was popular in 17th- and 18th-century England, distinguished by the use of egg for a rich, frothy texture. Port\'s deep sweetness paired with nutmeg makes it a short drink with a long, lingering finish, good as a digestif.',
  'kir': 'This cocktail is named after Canon Félix Kir, mayor of a town in France\'s Burgundy region after World War II, who is said to have favored serving it to guests. The tart sweetness of crème de cassis blends with white wine for an easy, light drink.',
  'golden-cadillac': 'As the story goes, this cocktail was born in the 1950s at a restaurant in California when a bartender recreated a combination a customer ordered without naming its ingredients. Vanilla-scented Galliano, white crème de cacao, and cream come together for a drink that holds its own as a dessert cocktail.',
  'pink-squirrel': 'This dessert cocktail is said to have caught on at a bar in Milwaukee, Wisconsin in the 1950s and \'60s. Amaretto, white crème de cacao, and cream come together for a nutty sweetness that makes it a lovely way to close out a meal.',
  'sombrero': 'This simple, smooth-style cocktail was popular in the United States in the 1970s and \'80s. Just coffee liqueur and cream come together for a rich, sweet drink that works well as a stand-in for dessert.',
  'colorado-bulldog': 'A cola-topped spin on the White Russian, a casual style that spread widely across the US from the 1970s onward. The rich sweetness of coffee and cream gets a fizzy lift from cola, making it almost dessert-like.',
  'lemon-drop-shot': 'This party shot is said to have originated in bars in San Francisco in the 1970s. The sugared rim and bright lemon make it feel like sipping a little lemon candy, a fun way to kick off a night out.',
  'buttery-nipple': 'This layered shot was popular in American bar culture in the 1990s, built from smooth, caramel-scented butterscotch schnapps stacked with Irish cream. Sweet as dessert, it\'s a great pick for livening up a party or get-together.',
  'mind-eraser': 'A layered shot popular in American bars through the 1980s and \'90s, known for its distinctive way of being drunk — straight through a straw, from the bottom of the glass, as fast as possible. Coffee liqueur, vodka, and soda water settle into visible layers, making it as fun to look at as it is to drink.',
  'washington-apple': 'A fairly recent shot cocktail that caught on in American college and party culture in the 2000s. Whiskey is rounded out with apple liqueur and cranberry juice for a tart-sweet, easy-drinking shot.',
}
